  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nThe upper system responsible for the storms and the severe weather \nrisk on Monday (that fortunately didn't materialize in our forecast \narea) will be slow to shift to the east today as it becomes more \nnegatively tilted over northeastern Kansas. As the upper low pivots \nthis morning, low-level flow will wrap into the surface low with \nlocally backed surface winds over northeastern Kansas and \nnorthwestern Missouri. Forecast soundings show modest, albeit \nsomewhat shallow CAPE with little or no inhibition by late this \nmorning/early this afternoon. This may result in low-topped \nconvection and given the very strong shear may lead to low-topped \nsupercells late this morning to early this afternoon. This risk \nappears greatest just northeast of the apparent triple point from \nfar northeastern Kansas into far northwestern Missouri. There are \nsome caveats with this. 1) This system is almost vertically stacked, \nwhich is less favorable than having an offset surface low and triple \npoint of about 100-200 miles. 2) shear is extremely strong and \nmostly unidirectional, which may just rip apart updrafts. \n\nFor the remainder of the week and into the weekend, cooler but \nmore seasonable conditions settle into the area. Normal highs \nfor the area now in the upper 50s and our forecast shows highs \nwithin a few degrees of our normals through the weekend. The \nrest of forecast looks dry with no notable systems moving \nthrough the area through Saturday. The GFS continues to show a \nrobust wave diving into the eastern half of the CONUS. This is \nconsiderably further east than yesterday and by early next week,\nupper ridging begins to build into the area, leading to \ntemperatures trending above normal.",
